{"meta": {"course": "cat100-supplement", "chapter": "03", "section": "05", "chapter_title": "Data Storytelling With Excel", "title": "AI Help for Excel Debugging", "description": "CAT 100 textbook section connecting ai help for excel debugging to Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.", "learning_objectives": ["Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.", "Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.", "Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.", "Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."], "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel", "cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "evidence_based_revision", "ai_supported_learning"], "estimated_time_minutes": 14, "order": 5, "top_tier_hardened": false, "prereq_section_ids": ["cat100-supplement/03/04"], "practice_ids": ["cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_artifact_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_annotation_signal", "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_ai_boundary", "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_calibration"]}, "content": "# AI Help for Excel Debugging\n\n![AI Help for Excel Debugging textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/03-05-ai-help-for-excel-debugging.png)\n\nAn AI assistant can write an Excel formula faster than you can type it, but it cannot know whether the formula is right for your data. It does not see your sheet; it only sees the words in your prompt. So the value you get from AI debugging depends almost entirely on two things you control: how precisely you describe the problem, and how rigorously you verify the answer before you trust it.\n\nThis section treats AI as a fast assistant that is sometimes confidently wrong. The discipline it teaches is a loop: read the actual error code to diagnose the cause, write a prompt that names the exact cells and the behavior you want, then verify the returned formula on a row whose answer you already know. Skipping the diagnosis or the verification is how a plausible-looking AI formula ships a hidden bug.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nA cell in your gradebook shows `#DIV/0!` and you are stuck. You paste \"fix my Excel error\" into an AI chat and it returns a formula that uses functions you do not recognize, applied to cells it guessed at. You drop it in, the error disappears, and you move on, never noticing that the formula now reports the wrong average for half the class.\n\nThe error went away, but the bug did not. That is the danger of using AI without diagnosis and verification: it is very good at making errors disappear and only sometimes good at making them correct. The move this section builds is the opposite habit, reading what `#DIV/0!` actually means, telling the AI exactly which cells and which behavior, and checking its answer against a row you can compute yourself.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nEffective AI debugging rests on two skills: reading Excel's error codes and writing prompts that leave nothing to guess. Excel's errors are not random; each one names a category of cause, so the error code is your first diagnostic clue.\n\n| Error | Means | Usual cause |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `#DIV/0!` | Division by zero | The denominator cell is blank or zero |\n| `#REF!` | Invalid reference | A cell the formula pointed to was deleted |\n| `#VALUE!` | Wrong data type | Text where a number is expected (`18 units`) |\n| `#NAME?` | Unrecognized name | A misspelled function or undefined range name |\n| `#N/A` | Value not found | A lookup found no match |\n\nReading the code tells you where to look before you ask anyone, human or AI, for help.\n\nA good debugging prompt has four parts: the current formula, the error or wrong result it produces, the cause if you know it, and the exact behavior you want, including any edge case. Compare \"fix my formula\" with \"My formula `=B4/D4` returns #DIV/0! because D4 is blank; I want it to show 'n/a' when D4 is empty and otherwise divide normally; give me one formula.\" The second prompt constrains the AI so tightly that its answer is easy to check. Then comes the non-negotiable step: verify the returned formula on a known-answer row, a row where you already know the correct result, before copying it anywhere.\n\n## Worked Example\n\nSuppose a class-average sheet shows an error in C4.\n\n```\n   A         B        C          D\n1  Student   Points   Per Item   ItemCount\n2  Maria     45       9.0        5\n3  David     45       9.0        5\n4  Sun       30       #DIV/0!    (blank)\n```\n\nThe formula in column C is `=B2/D2` (Points divided by ItemCount). ItemCount lives in column D, which is blank for Sun's row, so Excel divides by zero and column C shows `#DIV/0!`.\n\nStep 1. Read the error code, do not paper over it. `#DIV/0!` always means a divide-by-zero, so the denominator is the suspect, not the numerator.\n\nStep 2. Write a precise prompt for the AI assistant: \"My formula `=B4/D4` returns #DIV/0! because D4 is blank. I want it to show 'n/a' when D4 is empty and otherwise divide normally. Give me one formula.\"\n\nStep 3. The AI returns `=IF(D4=0,\"n/a\",B4/D4)`. Do not trust it blindly. Verify on David's row, where D3 = 5 and B3 = 45 should give 9.0. The new formula returns `45/5 = 9.0`, which matches the value already there. Now it is verified, not assumed.\n\nStep 4. Copy the verified formula down. C4 now reads `n/a` instead of an error.\n\nThe auditable trace: the error code named the cause, the prompt named the exact formula and expected behavior, and a known-answer row confirmed the AI fix before it shipped.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the diagnose -> prompt -> verify loop on #DIV/0!\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"Cell C4 shows #DIV/0!. What does the error code tell you to suspect first?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"The numerator B4 is wrong\",\"The denominator is blank or zero (division by zero)\",\"A function name is misspelled\",\"A lookup found no match\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"#DIV/0! always means divide-by-zero, so the denominator (D4, blank for Sun\u2019s row) is the suspect, not the numerator.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Which prompt gives the AI enough to return a checkable formula?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"fix my Excel error\",\"make my gradebook work\",\"My =B4/D4 returns #DIV/0! because D4 is blank; show n/a when D4 is empty, else divide; give one formula\",\"why is my sheet broken\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"A good prompt names the formula, the error, the cause, and the exact behavior including the edge case. That constrains the answer enough to verify.\"},{\"prompt\":\"The AI returns =IF(D4=0,\\\"n/a\\\",B4/D4). Before copying it down, what do you do?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Copy it everywhere immediately\",\"Verify on a known-answer row: David (45/5) must still return 9.0\",\"Trust it because the red error vanished\",\"Paste the whole gradebook into the chat\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Verify on a row whose answer you already know. David\u2019s row returns 45/5 = 9.0, matching the value already there, so the fix did not break working rows.\"},{\"reveal\":\"Only now copy it down: C4 reads n/a instead of an error. The error disappearing is NOT proof of correctness; a known-answer row is.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nMatch each Excel error code to its usual cause so you can diagnose before prompting.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"match\" items='[{\"term\":\"#DIV/0!\",\"definition\":\"Denominator cell is blank or zero\"},{\"term\":\"#REF!\",\"definition\":\"A referenced cell was deleted\"},{\"term\":\"#VALUE!\",\"definition\":\"Text where a number is expected (18 units)\"},{\"term\":\"#NAME?\",\"definition\":\"Misspelled function or undefined range name\"},{\"term\":\"#N/A\",\"definition\":\"A lookup found no match\"}]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nDiagnose with the error code, prompt with exact cells and behavior, and verify on a known-answer row before you copy anything. An AI formula is a suggestion until you have proven it on a value you can compute yourself.\n\nRun every AI fix through this gate:\n\n1. **Diagnosis check:** Did you read the actual error code and form a guess at the cause before asking?\n2. **Prompt check:** Does your prompt name the formula, the wrong result, and the exact behavior including edge cases?\n3. **Verification check:** Did the AI formula produce the correct, already-known answer on a test row before you trusted it?\n\nIf you cannot point to a row where you confirmed the AI formula was right, you have not finished debugging.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nThe biggest misreading is treating \"the error disappeared\" as proof the formula is correct. An AI fix that replaces `#DIV/0!` with a number can still compute the wrong number; the absence of a red error is not the presence of a right answer. Only a known-answer row settles it.\n\nA second misreading is pasting a whole sheet of sensitive data into a chat to get help. The AI does not need the data to fix a formula; it needs the formula, the error, and the intended behavior. Describe the structure and share the formula, not the people's names, grades, or other private values.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nApply the loop to a lookup error in a price list. Column C uses `=VLOOKUP(B2, Prices, 2, FALSE)` to fetch a price for each product code in B, but a few rows show `#N/A`. The error code is your first clue: `#N/A` means the lookup found no match, so the suspect is the lookup value, not the table.\n\nDiagnose before prompting. Inspect a failing row and you notice B7 reads `A-100 ` with a trailing space while the price table lists `A-100`; the space breaks the match. Now write a precise prompt: \"My `=VLOOKUP(B2, Prices, 2, FALSE)` returns #N/A on rows where the code has a trailing space; I want it to match after trimming spaces; give me one formula.\" The AI returns `=VLOOKUP(TRIM(B2), Prices, 2, FALSE)`.\n\nVerify before trusting. On a row you know is correct, where B3 is a clean `A-200` priced at \\$15, the new formula must still return 15. It does, so the TRIM did not break the working rows. Now copy it down and the `#N/A` rows resolve.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader treats an AI formula the way a careful editor treats a draft: useful, but unverified until checked. The habits to build:\n\n1. Read the error code first and predict the cause before opening a chat.\n2. Notice when a prompt is too vague to produce a checkable answer, and tighten it.\n3. Always identify a known-answer row to test the fix against.\n\nWhen your instinct on receiving any AI formula is \"let me test it on a row I can compute,\" you have internalized the safeguard that makes AI help trustworthy.\n\n<inline-check question=\"An AI formula replaced #DIV/0! with a real number and the red error is gone. Is the formula now proven correct?\" explanation=\"No. The absence of an error is not the presence of a right answer; an AI fix can compute the wrong number with no error showing. Only running it on a known-answer row, where you already know the correct result, settles whether it is right.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Yes, the error disappearing means it is correct\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, verify it on a known-answer row before trusting it\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, as long as the function names look familiar\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging\", \"cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Stronger Artifact Standard\n\nA strong AI-debugging artifact shows the whole loop, not just the final formula. It records the original broken formula and its error code, the exact prompt you wrote, the formula the AI returned, and the known-answer row that confirmed it. A model line reads: \"`=B4/D4` gave #DIV/0! on blank-denominator rows; I prompted for an n/a-on-empty version; the AI returned `=IF(D4=0,\"n/a\",B4/D4)`; I confirmed it returns 9.0 on David's row (45/5) before copying it down.\"\n\nThe weak version says \"AI helped me fix the formula.\" The strong version lets a reviewer see what was wrong, what was asked, what was returned, and the proof it was right, so the AI's contribution is bounded and checkable rather than a black box.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe same diagnose-prompt-verify loop transfers from formulas to chart and pivot problems. Suppose a pivot total looks too low and you suspect the source range. You would not paste the workbook into a chat; you would describe the symptom (\"my pivot of a 200-row log only totals about half the orders\"), state your hypothesis (\"I think the source range stops at row 100\"), and ask how to confirm and extend the range. Then you verify by hand-counting a subset against the refreshed pivot. The tool changed from a formula to a pivot, but the discipline, name the symptom, hypothesize the cause, verify the fix, is identical, which is the sign you have learned the loop rather than one formula.\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/03-05-ai-help-for-excel-debugging-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"03.05\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **[NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title for CAT 100 03.05. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students). Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"0\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the Excel pivot-table finding with unit labels, source note, and claim-first chart title records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- [NACE career readiness](https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined)\n- [ISTE digital citizenship](https://iste.org/standards/students)\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- [WCAG accessibility](https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/)\n", "simulation": null, "illustration": null, "practice": {"problems": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_artifact_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "cat100_supplement_data_storytelling_with_excel"], "stem": "Cell C2 shows #DIV/0! from the formula =A2/B2. What does this error tell you about the values?", "options": ["The formula is too long for the cell.", "B2 is empty or zero, and dividing by zero is undefined.", "A2 contains text instead of a number.", "The cell needs to be formatted as currency."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "#DIV/0! appears when a formula divides by an empty or zero cell. The fix is to handle the zero case, for example with =IF(B2=0,\"n/a\",A2/B2).", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_error_codes", "difficulty": "easy"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_annotation_signal", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging"], "stem": "A VLOOKUP returns #N/A for some lookup values that you can see in the source table. The most reliable thing to check first is:", "options": ["Whether the workbook needs to be saved again.", "Whether the lookup value has extra spaces or differs in type (text vs number) from the value in the source column.", "Whether the column is wide enough to show the result.", "Whether the font color matches the table."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "#N/A from a lookup usually means no exact match was found. Trailing spaces or a text-vs-number mismatch between the lookup value and the source column is the most common cause.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_error_codes", "difficulty": "medium"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_ai_boundary", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "ai_supported_learning"], "stem": "You paste a broken formula into an AI tool and it returns a confident rewrite. Before trusting it, what is the responsible verification step?", "options": ["Submit the rewrite immediately because the AI sounded certain.", "Test the rewritten formula on a row where you already know the correct answer, then confirm it matches.", "Ask the AI again until two answers agree.", "Use the rewrite only if it is shorter than yours."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "AI can return a fluent but wrong formula. Checking it against a row whose answer you already know turns the suggestion into a verified fix rather than a guess.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_unverified_ai_fix", "difficulty": "hard"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_calibration", "type": "multiple_choice", "concept_id": "cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "concept_ids": ["cat100_supplement_ai_help_for_excel_debugging", "evidence_based_revision"], "stem": "To get useful debugging help from an AI assistant, the most effective thing to include in your prompt is:", "options": ["Only the word 'help' and the file name.", "The exact formula, the error message it produces, and what result you expected for a specific row.", "A request to rewrite the entire spreadsheet.", "The color theme you used in the workbook."], "correct_index": 1, "explanation": "A precise debugging prompt gives the formula text, the error message, and the expected result for a concrete row. Vague prompts produce vague or wrong fixes.", "misconception_id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_vague_prompt", "difficulty": "medium"}]}, "content_version": {"content_version": "5a7b4d3dc461aae048d5453b889991bfb017c4e422012e5b2cd2427449eebe56", "algorithm": "sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1", "parts": ["mdx", "meta", "practice", "misconceptions"]}, "misconceptions": [{"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_error_codes", "pattern": "ignoring_error_code_meaning", "description": "Treating Excel error codes like #DIV/0! and #N/A as random glitches instead of specific signals about the data or formula", "trigger": "learner sees an error code and tries cosmetic fixes like widening the column", "feedback": "Read the error code as a clue. #DIV/0! means a divide-by-zero, and #N/A from a lookup means no match was found, often from spaces or a text-vs-number mismatch.", "rail_action": "explain"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_unverified_ai_fix", "pattern": "trusting_unverified_ai_fix", "description": "Accepting an AI-suggested formula rewrite as correct because it sounds confident, without testing it against a known answer", "trigger": "learner pastes an AI rewrite back into the sheet without verifying it", "feedback": "Test the AI rewrite on a row whose correct answer you already know. Confidence is not correctness.", "rail_action": "represent"}, {"id": "cat100_supplement_3_5_ai_help_for_excel_debugging_vague_prompt", "pattern": "vague_debugging_prompt", "description"